---
// biome-ignore lint/correctness/noUnusedImports: Astro uses this layout component below.
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="FAQ | plansplease"
  description="Answers about the plansplease CLI, cloud workspace, privacy, and alpha plans."
  activePath="faq"
>
  <section class="page-heading" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <div>
      <h1 id="faq-title">Questions we expect to hear.</h1>
      <p>
        Clear, direct answers about how plansplease serves pages locally, keeps a cloud
        copy, and fits agent workflows.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-content">
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>
          <span>What is plansplease?</span>
          <span class="faq-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            plansplease gives agent-generated web artifacts a saved copy you can reopen. Coding
            agents (Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT, Gemini) often output complete single-page tools,
            simulators, or dashboards.
          </p>
          <p>
            The plansplease CLI creates a local preview on your machine,
            while our cloud workspace saves your pages so they do not get lost in a
            closed chat thread.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>What file types and assets can I preview?</span>
          <span class="faq-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The local CLI serves both standalone HTML files and multi-file directory bundles with an
            <code>index.html</code> entry point. You can include linked stylesheets, images, client-side
            JavaScript, Canvas scripts, and CDN-hosted libraries (like Tailwind, Chart.js, or React).
          </p>
          <p>
            The cloud workspace currently accepts standalone HTML files with full support for inline
            scripts and CDN dependencies.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>Why not just open the HTML file with my browser?</span>
          <span class="faq-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Browser security restrictions on <code>file://</code> URLs can prevent module imports,
            asset requests, and web workers from working as expected.
          </p>
          <p>
            plansplease runs a minimal loopback daemon on <code>127.0.0.1</code> that serves your
            files over HTTP, so relative assets work without starting a development server.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>How does plansplease connect with Claude Code or Cursor?</span>
          <span class="faq-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Use the CLI from your agent's terminal, or connect the <a href="../mcp/">hosted MCP server</a>.
            After browser sign-in and consent, MCP can list, read, upload, and delete your cloud documents.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>Are cloud files private?</span>
          <span class="faq-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            Local previews stay on your machine's loopback interface. Cloud uploads require GitHub
            sign-in, and the app lists only your account's documents. The underlying
            UploadThing files are public-read during alpha: anyone with a direct file URL can fetch
            one. Do not upload sensitive content.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>
          <span>Can I read the source code?</span>
          <span class="faq-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>
            The app, CLI, local daemon, and storage implementation are available in the
            <a href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/plansplease">GitHub repository</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <p class="page-note">
      Have a question that is not covered here? Open an issue on
      <a href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/plansplease">GitHub</a> or visit the
      <a href="../docs/">documentation page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .faq-content {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .faq-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .faq-item {
    border: 1px solid var(--night-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--night-card);
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: border-color 160ms ease;
  }

  .faq-item:hover,
  .faq-item[open] {
    border-color: var(--night-border-hover);
  }

  .faq-item summary {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.35rem 1.6rem;
    color: var(--text-white);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    font-weight: 550;
    cursor: pointer;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-toggle-icon {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--night-subtle);
    transition: transform 180ms ease, color 180ms ease;
  }

  .faq-item[open] .faq-toggle-icon {
    transform: rotate(45deg);
    color: var(--mint);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    padding: 0 1.6rem 1.5rem;
    color: var(--night-muted);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--night-border);
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .faq-answer p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .faq-answer p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
